%N A397152 Number of partitions of {1,...,3*n} into 3-element subsets {x,y,z} satisfying x+y=3*z.
%C A397152 a(n) = 0 unless n == 0 or 5 (mod 8).
%C A397152 More generally, let a_c(n) count partitions satisfying x+y=c*z, where c is a positive integer. For c=5, a_c(n)=1 for n=0,1 and a_c(n)=0 for n>=2; the assertion for n>=2 is proved in Thomas Andrews' answer linked below. For every c>=6, a_c(0)=1 and a_c(n)=0 for n>=1. Indeed, since x and y are distinct elements of {1,...,3*n}, we have x+y<=3*n+(3*n-1)=6*n-1, and hence z<=floor((6*n-1)/c)<=n-1. Thus the n distinct values of z would all have to belong to {1,...,n-1}, which is impossible.
%H A397152 Thomas Andrews, <a href="https://math.stackexchange.com/questions/4195206/split-1-3n-into-triples-with-xy-5z-no-solutions">Answer to "Split {1,...,3n} into triples with x+y=5z - no solutions?"</a> (the case c=5).
%e A397152 {{{1,4,11},{2,5,13},{3,6,15},{7,9,12},{8,10,14}},
%e A397152  {{1,5,14},{2,4,10},{3,6,15},{7,9,12},{8,11,13}},
%e A397152  {{1,3,8},{2,5,13},{4,6,14},{7,10,11},{9,12,15}},
%e A397152  {{1,4,11},{2,3,7},{5,6,13},{8,10,14},{9,12,15}}}
%e A397152 are the 4 ways for n=5.
